guide='''<div class="reading"><div class="reading-tools"><a href="/resources/">← Teaching resources</a><button type="button" data-print>Print / Save as PDF</button></div><p class="eyebrow">Discussion guide · 40 minutes</p><h1>Walking in the light</h1><p class="lede">1 John 1:5–2:2 · Read the passage in your Bible before beginning.</p><div class="study-note"><p><strong>Aim:</strong> Attend to John’s account of God’s light, honest confession, fellowship, and Christ’s advocacy. Let the passage guide the conversation from careful observation toward faithful response.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>1. Read and notice · 8 minutes</h2><p>Read 1 John 1:5–2:2 aloud. Notice the claims introduced by “if,” the changes between “we” and “you,” and the references to Christ.</p><ul><li>What does John say about God in 1:5?</li><li>Which claims does he challenge in 1:6, 1:8, and 1:10?</li><li>What does he promise in 1:7 and 1:9?</li></ul><h2>2. Follow the argument · 12 minutes</h2><ul><li>How does John relate walking in the light to fellowship and cleansing?</li><li>How does confession differ from claiming to be without sin?</li><li>Why should we read 2:1–2 alongside 1:5–10? What does Christ’s advocacy contribute to John’s counsel?</li></ul><p>Keep each answer close to the wording and movement of the passage. Where an interpretation depends on an inference, distinguish it from what John explicitly states.</p><h2>3. Consider the shared life · 10 minutes</h2><ul><li>What might honest fellowship look like in a class, small group, or congregation?</li><li>What habits make it difficult to acknowledge sin truthfully?</li><li>How can a community receive confession with care while taking repentance seriously?</li></ul><h2>4. Reflect and respond · 6 minutes</h2><p>Take a quiet moment to identify one truth in the passage that needs to shape your response this week. What would that response involve, and who might help you practice it?</p><div class="study-note"><p><strong>For the leader:</strong> Invite reflection without requiring public disclosure of personal sins. Give participants room to consider a suitable pastoral conversation privately.</p></div><h2>5. Pray · 4 minutes</h2><p>Give thanks for the character of God and the advocacy of Christ. Pray for truthful confession, faithful fellowship, and a shared life that welcomes the light.</p><h2>Return to the passage</h2><p>Read it again later in the week. Revisit one observation, one question, and one response. Notice what becomes clearer through returning.</p></div></div>'''
page('Walking in the light','A printable 40-minute discussion guide for 1 John 1:5–2:2.',guide,'resources/walking-in-the-light')
(OUT/'downloads').mkdir(exist_ok=True)
download=(OUT/'resources/walking-in-the-light/index.html').read_text().replace('<title>Walking in the light | Habura Research</title>','<title>Walking in the light: printable guide</title>')
(OUT/'downloads/walking-in-the-light.html').write_text(download)

body=intro('About the author','Scholarship in service<br>of the church.','Craig R. Fredrickson is an independent scholar, writer, and adult Bible teacher based in South Carolina.')+'''<div class="content-grid wrap"><aside class="side-note"><div class="portrait-letter" aria-hidden="true">C.</div><p class="eyebrow">Craig R. Fredrickson</p><p>Biblical theology<br>Exegesis<br>Christian formation</p></aside><div class="prose"><h2>A life of reading and teaching</h2><p>Craig’s work brings close biblical interpretation into conversation with the life of the church. His interests center on how Scripture describes God’s people: their redemption, fellowship, worship, obligations, and hope.</p><p>He has spent approximately fifteen years teaching adult Bible classes. His writing seeks to serve both the demands of careful scholarship and the questions people bring into a room where Scripture is being read together.</p><p>Before retirement, his professional life focused on executive leadership, operational excellence, and continuous improvement. He now devotes his attention to research, writing, and teaching.</p><h2>Why Habura?</h2><p>The name takes its cue from the Hebrew term for a company or fellowship. It reflects a recurring question in the research: how does God’s saving work gather a people and shape their life together?</p><p>Habura Research gives that inquiry a home through published scholarship, commentary projects, reflective essays, and resources for the church.</p><h2>Rooted in Scripture</h2><p>Careful reading and spiritual formation belong together in this work. The aim is to help readers attend to what Scripture says and allow its teaching to shape love, faith, conduct, and worship.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/research/">Read the publications</a><a class="text-link" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a></div></div></div>'''
page('About Craig R. Fredrickson','Independent scholar, writer, and Bible teacher Craig R. Fredrickson explores Scripture and the life of the church.',body,'about',{'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'ProfilePage','url':ORIGIN+'/about/','mainEntity':{'@type':'Person','name':'Craig R. Fredrickson','description':'Independent scholar, writer, and adult Bible teacher based in South Carolina.','url':ORIGIN+'/about/'}})

body=intro('Contact','Begin a conversation.','For research correspondence, teaching and speaking inquiries, or questions about the writing projects.')+f'''<section class="contact-grid wrap"><div class="prose"><h2>Research & writing</h2><p>Questions about a published article, its argument, or the broader research program are welcome. Include the article title or passage so the conversation can begin with the right context.</p><h2>Teaching & speaking</h2><p>For a church class, reading group, or speaking invitation, include the setting, topic, and dates you have in mind.</p></div><div class="contact-panel"><p class="eyebrow">Contact Craig</p><h2>Send an email.</h2><p class="email-address">{EMAIL}</p><div class="actions">{link('Open your email app','mailto:'+EMAIL,'button')}</div><p>Prefer a contact form? You can use the existing form on Spiraling Light.</p>{link('Open the contact form ↗','https://www.spiralinglight.com/about-me')}</div></section>'''
page('Contact','Research correspondence, teaching inquiries, and questions about Craig R. Fredrickson’s writing.',body,'contact')

